  <h2 id="college">The college and degree filter for PCMB students</h2>

  <p>
    Some PCMB-shaped routes are degree-heavy and lab-dependent. Others reward projects and self-built proof more than the college name.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">College matters more when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You are aiming for MBBS, BDS, or a formally licensed clinical route.</li>
        <li>The biomedical or bioinformatics programme needs specific lab access, faculty, or accreditation.</li>
        <li>The early hiring filters in that specific field still weight the degree route heavily.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--amber">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Skill and proof matter more when</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You are aiming for health-tech, medical software, or general engineering roles that reward projects and portfolios.</li>
        <li>You can build visible proof — a project, an analysis, a working demo — outside the classroom.</li>
        <li>The college is average and your edge must come from what you build around it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Use roughly 10% of your total education budget as a conservative starting point for the college fee itself, leaving the rest for tools, projects, targeted exam prep, and future upskilling — treat this as a planning heuristic, not a fixed rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spend materially more only when a specific institution offers a verified, course-specific advantage — real lab access, strong placement outcomes, or genuine faculty and peer quality — not general prestige alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="drop-decision">If you are already struggling mid-way through PCMB</h2>

  <p>
    This happens to a real share of PCMB students, and it is not a crisis if you act on it early.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Name which subject is actually slipping.</strong>
      {' '}
      Be specific — a dropping test score in one subject over several months is a signal, not a bad week.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check whether you can still recover it with a focused plan.</strong>
      {' '}
      Sometimes a subject slips because of a gap in one topic area, not a fundamental mismatch. A tutor or a focused revision block can fix that.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If it does not recover, drop early rather than late.</strong>
      {' '}
      A drop made in early or mid-11th protects your other three subjects. A drop forced by panic in 12th costs you far more.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reframe the drop as a decision, not a defeat.</strong>
      {' '}
      You tested both sides honestly and chose one with real evidence. That is exactly what the extra subject was for.
    </li>
  </ol>

